---
import "../../styles/v2.css";
import "../../styles/ck.css";
import "../../styles/methodology.css";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { paintSpec } from "../../lib/chartkit";
import { brandTheme } from "../../data/marketing-theme";
import {
  TOKEN_CANNOT_DRAW,
  TOKEN_CHART_TYPES,
  TOKEN_DOCS,
  TOKEN_EVAL,
  TOKEN_LIBRARIES,
  formatEvalDate,
  formatTokens,
  tasksPerChartType,
  tokenScoreboard,
  tokenScoreboardSpec,
  tokenSpread,
} from "../../data/token-eval";

const perType = tasksPerChartType();
const run = TOKEN_EVAL.run;
const published = formatEvalDate(run.publishedOn);
const spread = tokenSpread();
const chartkitMedian = spread.rows.find((row) => row.us);
const nextMedian = spread.rows.find((row) => !row.us);
if (!chartkitMedian || !nextMedian) throw new Error("token spread is missing a row");
const board = tokenScoreboard();
const finished = board.rows.filter((row) => !row.pending);
const chartkitDoc = TOKEN_DOCS.find((row) => row.library === "ChartKit");
if (!chartkitDoc) throw new Error("token eval is missing the ChartKit doc");

const spec = {
  ...tokenScoreboardSpec(),
  theme: brandTheme,
  size: { width: 960, height: 700, padding: { right: 28, left: 8, bottom: 4 } },
};
const painted = paintSpec(spec);
if (!painted.ok || !painted.svg.includes("<svg") || painted.svg.includes("ck-chart-error")) {
  throw new Error(
    `token scoreboard chart failed: ${painted.footer} ${painted.issues.map((issue) => issue.message).join("; ")}`,
  );
}
const svg = painted.svg.includes('class="ck-chart"')
  ? painted.svg.replace('class="ck-chart"', 'class="ck-svg ck-chart"')
  : painted.svg.replace("<svg ", '<svg class="ck-svg" ');

const versionLine = run.versionRecorded
  ? `The run recorded ChartKit ${run.chartkitVersion}.`
  : `The run did not record a ChartKit version. ${run.chartkitVersion} is the version in the ChartKit package on ${published}, the day these medians were published.`;

const description = `How the agent token test was run. ChartKit ${run.chartkitVersion}, published ${published}. Median output tokens on ${TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} tasks across ${finished.length} models, with docs.`;
---

<Base tone="light" title="Agent token test" description={description}>
  <div class="ck ck-wrap">
    <article class="method">
      <p class="ck-eye">Methodology</p>
      <h1 class="ck-h1">How the agent token test was run</h1>
      <p class="ck-lede">
        The homepage chart is this test. A model is asked for the same {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} charts in five libraries, with a
        capped copy of that library's docs in the prompt. The number is the median output tokens until the checker accepts
        a drawing.
      </p>
      <p class="method-meta">
        ChartKit {run.chartkitVersion} · {published} · {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} tasks · {finished.length} models · with docs
      </p>
      <p>
        {versionLine} The package version is still {run.chartkitVersion}, and the library has changed since that day, so
        today's package is not this run. {run.docDrift} The cap is {formatTokens(run.docCapBytes)} bytes.
      </p>

      <section class="caveat" id="caveats" aria-labelledby="caveats-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="caveats-title">Caveats</h2>
        <p class="caveat-lead">Read these before treating the chart as a ranking of libraries.</p>
        <div class="caveat-list">
          <div>
            <h3>We wrote the library and the test</h3>
            <p>
              ChartKit's authors wrote the tasks, the prompts, the checker, and the length cap. Nobody outside the project
              scored this run.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>The task set is ours, and it is small</h3>
            <p>
              {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} tasks, {perType} each of {TOKEN_EVAL.chartTypes} chart types, each with a few rows we wrote
              for the harness. This is not a sample of production charts, and it is not a suite someone else published.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Five libraries, chosen as the harness set</h3>
            <p>
              The scoreboard is ChartKit, Observable Plot, Chart.js, Recharts, and ECharts. uPlot, Nivo, and the commercial
              libraries in the paint benchmarks are not in this test. The bench pinned {run.rivalVersions.map((row) => `${row.name} ${row.version}`).join(", ")} on the day the figures were published. The result file does not record which versions were installed when the models ran.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>The docs are not the same kind of document</h3>
            <p>
              ChartKit's prompt includes the whole of llms.txt, written as instructions for an agent. The other libraries
              get the opening of a getting-started guide, cut so it is no longer than that file. Cutting them to our
              length was our choice. We did not measure what any model had already seen in training. A model that already
              knows Chart.js is not in the same position as a model reading ChartKit's spec for the first time, and this
              test does not separate those cases.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>A token here is the provider's count</h3>
            <p>
              There is no shared tokenizer. Anthropic reports output tokens. OpenAI and xAI chat responses report
              completion tokens. Those units are not the same, and the chart still puts them on one axis. A .5 in the
              table is the average of the two middle values when the number of working charts is even. It is not a
              fraction of a token from the provider.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Tokens are not quality, speed, or cost</h3>
            <p>
              The table is output tokens on charts the checker accepted. It says nothing about how the picture looks to a
              person, how fast the library draws, or how large the bundle is. The harness has placeholder prices, and
              this page does not publish a dollar figure. Input tokens are stored by the harness and are not in the table.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Correct means the checker accepted it</h3>
            <p>
              Nobody graded the charts by eye, and there is no comparison against a reference picture. A hand-written
              drawing fails. A short wrong answer fails and drops out of the median, so a low number is only the cost of
              the answers that passed.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Unfinished tasks leave the median</h3>
            <p>
              A task that never passes is listed and left out. The median is not recorded as infinity. A median over a
              handful of successes can look tidy. The published notes say which rows that applies to. The other models do
              not publish a working-chart count, so their cells do not show how many tasks they finished.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>Declining a chart is not drawing one</h3>
            <p>
              When a library has no such chart, an honest decline counts in the pass rate and is left out of the token
              median. Plot, Chart.js, and Recharts are expected to decline several of the {TOKEN_EVAL.chartTypes} types.
              That is the rule of the checker, not a result.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>One pass per model, and no measured variance</h3>
            <p>
              Temperature was {run.temperature}. Each model went through the task list once. There is at most one retry
              for a fixable error. Temperature {run.temperature} does not promise the provider will return the same text
              twice, and this test did not repeat a model, so there is no measured spread from randomness. The spread in
              the chart is the spread across models.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>These model ids, on this day</h3>
            <p>
              The figures are the registry ids below, published {published}. A provider can change a model without
              changing the id. ChartKit in the test is the {run.chartkitVersion} tree from that day.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3>What this page does not contain</h3>
            <p>
              The nine-model run without docs was not published. The harness can run that condition. A separate one-model
              scoreboard, with docs and without, is on the <a href="/benchmarks#agents">benchmarks page</a>. Those
              figures are not this table. This test also does not measure a multi-step agent, tool use, or charts outside
              these {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} tasks.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="results" aria-labelledby="results-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="results-title">Results</h2>
        <p>
          With docs. Each cell is the median output tokens for that model and library, counting only charts the checker
          accepted. Across the {spread.modelCount} models, the median of ChartKit's medians is {chartkitMedian.medianText} tokens.
          The next library is {nextMedian.name} at {nextMedian.medianText}. That ratio is {spread.headline}×. By model,
          ChartKit uses {spread.ratioLead} fewer output tokens than whichever library was next. ChartKit's published
          median model calls on a working chart are {spread.calls}. <code>repair()</code> does not add a call.
        </p>
        <figure class="method-chart">
          <div class="ck-frame" set:html={svg} />
          <figcaption>
            Median output tokens per working chart. ChartKit is the lowest bar on every model. Notes under the table flag
            a median that rests on a thin set of finished charts.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="compare-table-wrap">
          <table class="compare-table scoreboard">
            <caption class="sr">Median output tokens per working chart by model.</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Model</th>
                {
                  board.libraries.map((library) => (
                    <th class:list={["num", library === "ChartKit" && "us"]} scope="col">
                      {library}
                    </th>
                  ))
                }
                <th class="num" scope="col">CK vs next</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                board.rows.map((row) => (
                  <tr class:list={[row.pending && "is-pending"]}>
                    <th scope="row">{row.name}</th>
                    {row.cells.map((cell) => (
                      <td class:list={["num", cell.us && "us"]}>
                        {cell.text}
                        {cell.mark ? <sup>{cell.mark}</sup> : null}
                      </td>
                    ))}
                    <td class="num">{row.versus}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        {
          board.rows
            .filter((row) => row.notes.length > 0)
            .map((row) => (
              <p class="token-note">
                <strong>{row.name}.</strong>{" "}
                {row.notes.map((note) => (
                  <>
                    {note.mark ? <sup>{note.mark}</sup> : null}
                    {note.text}{" "}
                  </>
                ))}
              </p>
            ))
        }
        <p class="token-note">
          The homepage bars are the median of these {finished.length} rows. Did-not-finish runs are excluded from every
          cell. Pending models, if a later run adds one, stay out of the median and show as pending in this table.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="what" aria-labelledby="what-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="what-title">What we tested, and why</h2>
        <p>
          The question was how many output tokens a model spends to get a chart the checker will accept, when the answer
          is a ChartKit spec, and when the answer is code for another library. The homepage uses that comparison. The
          agents page is the product page for the loop. This page is the test.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each task is a short request plus a small data table: a line of monthly revenue, a bar of signups, a gauge at
          72, and so on. The model sees the request and the data. The checker already knows the chart type, the fields,
          and the numbers.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="models" aria-labelledby="models-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="models-title">Models and tasks</h2>
        <p>
          {finished.length} models finished. Temperature {run.temperature}. Maximum output tokens per response{" "}
          {formatTokens(run.maxOutputTokens)}. One pass through the task list per model. The registry id is the id the
          client sent.
        </p>
        <div class="compare-table-wrap">
          <table class="compare-table">
            <caption class="sr">Models in the published token test.</caption>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope="col">Name</th>
                <th scope="col">Registry id</th>
                <th scope="col">Provider</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {
                TOKEN_EVAL.models.map((model) => (
                  <tr>
                    <th scope="row">{model.group === "Claude" ? `Claude ${model.name}` : model.name}</th>
                    <td><code>{model.id}</code></td>
                    <td>{model.provider}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))
              }
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} tasks, {perType} of each type: {TOKEN_CHART_TYPES.join(", ")}. The libraries on the
          scoreboard are {TOKEN_LIBRARIES.join(", ")}.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="docs" aria-labelledby="docs-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="docs-title">With docs and without</h2>
        <p>
          Without docs, every library gets one short paragraph: how to produce a drawing, and where the fields go. The
          paragraphs do the same job at a similar length. ChartKit's paragraph does not add channel shapes the others
          lack.
        </p>
        <p>
          With docs, that paragraph stays, and the library's own text is placed in front of it. ChartKit's text is{" "}
          <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>, {formatTokens(chartkitDoc.bytes)} bytes, and that length is the cap. The
          others are cut to it, on a character boundary, and trimmed back to a line break when that still leaves most of
          the excerpt. The published table is this with-docs condition. The nine-model without-docs condition was not
          published.
        </p>
        <ul class="method-docs">
          {
            TOKEN_DOCS.map((row) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{row.library}.</strong> {formatTokens(row.bytes)} bytes. {row.summary}
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>
          The system line on every call is "You produce only the requested chart artifact. No prose." ChartKit is asked
          for one JSON object. The other libraries are asked for a module that imports that library and returns the
          drawing it produced.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="tokens" aria-labelledby="tokens-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="tokens-title">How the tokens were counted</h2>
        <p>
          The harness does not tokenize the text itself. It stores the count on the provider's response. When the
          response has <code>output_tokens</code>, that field is used. Otherwise <code>completion_tokens</code> is used.
          Anthropic's Messages API reports <code>output_tokens</code>. OpenAI and xAI chat responses report{" "}
          <code>completion_tokens</code>. A gpt-6 id that Chat Completions rejects is sent to OpenAI's Responses API,
          which reports <code>output_tokens</code>. The published rows do not say which of those two OpenAI paths
          answered.
        </p>
        <p>
          For a chart the checker accepted, the cell adds those output counts across the model calls that led there: the
          first answer, and the retry when there was one. <code>repair()</code> adds no tokens. An honest decline is not
          a working chart, so it stays out of the median. A task that never passes stays out too. The median of an odd
          count is the middle value. The median of an even count is the average of the two middle values.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="correct" aria-labelledby="correct-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="correct-title">How a chart counted as correct</h2>
        <p>
          A ChartKit answer has to validate, render as the chart type the task asked for, use the expected fields, and
          carry the task's numbers. The drawing is checked too: bars sit inside the plot, a line chart has a line, a
          donut has its slices, a scatter has its points, a gauge or progress ring has an arc, a KPI has the value.
          Gauge, KPI, and progress ring also have to put the number on the metric.
        </p>
        <p>
          A code answer has to import the library and call it. The type is read from the drawing, not from a note the
          model attaches. The numbers are read from the drawing the same way. Recharts has to produce an SVG with data
          marks, mounted in a browser. Chart.js has to paint pixels on a canvas. A canvas tag with a blank canvas fails.
          Observable Plot has to return the SVG from Plot.plot. ECharts can render to SVG on the server, and that SVG has
          to contain the marks. Code that touches the document runs in a browser instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where the library has no chart of that type, the prompt tells the model to decline. A substitute chart fails.
          The declines the checker expects:
        </p>
        <ul class="method-docs">
          {
            TOKEN_CANNOT_DRAW.map((row) => (
              <li>
                <strong>{row.library}.</strong> {row.types.join(", ")} ({row.types.length * perType} of {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks} tasks).
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p>ChartKit is asked for all {TOKEN_EVAL.tasks}.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="retries" aria-labelledby="retries-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="retries-title">Retries</h2>
        <p>
          ChartKit <code>repair()</code> runs locally on a failed answer. If the repaired spec passes, the model is not
          called again. That repair adds no call and no tokens.
        </p>
        <p>
          Otherwise the harness sends the failure back once, and only when the failure was validation, rendering,
          compiling, or not actually using the library. The retry includes the error text and the previous answer. A
          wrong chart type, or the wrong numbers, is not sent back.
        </p>
        <p>
          A request the network rejects, a rate limit, or a server error is retried up to five more times, with a pause
          that doubles from two seconds. Those are not extra attempts at the task. A failed request adds no tokens. The
          response that succeeds is counted once.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each of the {finished.length} models was run once. The table is that pass. {run.repeats === 1 ? "There is no second seed." : `${run.repeats} passes were aggregated.`}
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="version" aria-labelledby="version-title">
        <h2 class="ck-h2" id="version-title">ChartKit version and the day of the run</h2>
        <p>
          {versionLine} {run.publishNote}{" "}
          {run.resultsPublished
            ? "The raw per-model results are published with the site."
            : "The raw per-model result files are not published with the site, so there is no separate clock time from the model calls."}{" "}
          The harness scripts that score a chart are unchanged since that day. The ChartKit package they call has moved
          since, under the same version number, and these cells were not re-scored.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/agents">Agents</a> is the product page. <a href="/benchmarks#agents">Benchmarks</a> keeps the earlier
          one-model scoreboard.
        </p>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
